.TH MEH 1 "2013-12-28"
.SH NAME
meh \- simple, minimalist, super fast image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B meh
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B meh \-list
.RI [ LISTFILE ]
.br
.B meh \-ctl
.br
.B meh \-v
.br
.B meh \-h
.br
.B meh \-\-
.IR FILE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B meh
is a small, simple, super fast image viewer using raw XLib.
It is similar to
.BR feh (1),
but faster and simpler.
.LP
.B meh
can use ImageMagick's
.BR convert (1)
to view almost 200 file formats, though it is slower for these formats.
Built-in formats are JPEG, PNG, BMP, and netpbm.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-list \ [\fILISTFILE\fR]
Treat \fILISTFILE\fR as list of images. Defaults to stdin.
.TP
.B \-ctl
Display files as they are received on stdin.
.TP
.B \-v
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. Every remaining argument is treated as a filename even if it
begins with a dash, so a file called \fI\-a\fR can be viewed with
.BR "meh \-\- \-a" .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A first argument beginning with a dash that is not one of the options above
is rejected as an unknown option rather than taken for a filename. Use
.B \-\-
or a path such as \fI./\-a\fR to view such a file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B meh
exits 0 when it was asked to print its version or help, and non-zero when
it was invoked incorrectly or could not display any image. A usage summary
printed because of a bad invocation goes to standard error; one printed
because
.B \-h
asked for it goes to standard output.
